  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Prove that the diagonals of a kite are perpendicular by following the steps below.\n\nFirst, copy this diagram of a kite neatly into your book. Then answer the following questions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-diagram",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 22 p22-q3-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.8pt,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,text=black}]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\\coordinate (B) at (1.5,1.5);\\coordinate (C) at (4.5,0);\\coordinate (D) at (1.5,-1.5);\\coordinate (M) at (1.5,0);\n\\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;\\draw (A)--(C);\\draw (B)--(D);\n\\foreach \\P/\\Q in {A/B,A/D}{\\draw[line width=0.4pt] ($ (\\P)!0.5!(\\Q)!2pt!90:(\\Q) $)--($ (\\P)!0.5!(\\Q)!2pt!-90:(\\Q) $);}\n\\foreach \\P/\\Q in {B/C,D/C}{\\foreach \\t in {0.48,0.52}{\\draw[line width=0.4pt] ($ (\\P)!\\t!(\\Q)!2pt!90:(\\Q) $)--($ (\\P)!\\t!(\\Q)!2pt!-90:(\\Q) $);}}\n\\node[left] at (A) {$A$};\\node[above] at (B) {$B$};\\node[right] at (C) {$C$};\\node[below] at (D) {$D$};\\node[above right] at (M) {$M$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 56.8,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 22,
          "sourceRegion": "Left column, question 3",
          "description": "Kite with both diagonals, intersection M, single ticks on AB and AD, and double ticks on BC and CD. Decorative orange interior fill is omitted.",
          "mathematics": "AB=AD and BC=CD; AC and BD intersect at M. No right-angle marker is supplied.",
          "answerVisibility": "Perpendicularity remains to be proved."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\triangle ABC\\equiv\\triangle ADC$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\triangle ABC\\equiv\\triangle ADC$ by SSS: $AB=AD$, $BC=DC$ and $AC$ is shared.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AB&=AD &&\\text{(given)}\\\\BC&=DC &&\\text{(given)}\\\\AC&=AC &&\\text{(shared side)}\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $\\triangle ABC\\equiv\\triangle ADC$ by SSS.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why $\\angle BAM=\\angle DAM$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They are corresponding angles in the congruent triangles from (a), since $M$ lies on $AC$.",
          "worked": "From (a), corresponding angles give $\\angle BAC=\\angle DAC$. Since $M$ lies on $AC$, these are $\\angle BAM$ and $\\angle DAM$. Hence $\\angle BAM=\\angle DAM$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\triangle AMB\\equiv\\triangle AMD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\triangle AMB\\equiv\\triangle AMD$ by SAS: $AB=AD$, $AM$ is shared and $\\angle BAM=\\angle DAM$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AB&=AD &&\\text{(given)}\\\\AM&=AM &&\\text{(shared side)}\\\\\\angle BAM&=\\angle DAM &&\\text{(proved in (b))}\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $\\triangle AMB\\equiv\\triangle AMD$ by SAS.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Explain why $\\angle AMB=\\angle AMD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They are corresponding angles in the congruent triangles from (c).",
          "worked": "$\\triangle AMB\\equiv\\triangle AMD$ from (c). Their corresponding angles at $M$ are equal, so $\\angle AMB=\\angle AMD$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Why does this mean that $BD$ is perpendicular to $AC$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The equal adjacent angles sum to $180^\\circ$, so each is $90^\\circ$. Hence $BD\\perp AC$.",
          "worked": "$B$, $M$ and $D$ lie on a straight line, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle AMB+\\angle AMD&=180^\\circ\\\\2\\angle AMB&=180^\\circ\\\\\\angle AMB&=90^\\circ\\end{align*}$$\nThus the diagonals meet at a right angle, and $BD\\perp AC$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
